==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Hoppity Neneko''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Blossoming Neneko''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
Easter Neneko behaves similarly to [[Neneko (Super Rare Cat)|Neneko]], but as with all Neneko variants, she has received a notable change: a faster attack rate in exchange for a lower chance of critical. However, due to her lack of change in cooldown time, she remains as unreliable as ever and is outclassed by other notable critters, like [[Jurassic Cat (Rare Cat)|Catasaurus]], [[Catornado (Super Rare Cat)|Catornado]], and [[Cheerleader Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Waitress Cat]].

==='''True Form'''===
Blossoming Neneko has a chance to knockback, freeze, slow, or weaken any [[:Category:Metal Enemies|Metal]] enemies she hits. Unfortunately, each status effect only has a 15% chance, which means that her chance of getting any of these status conditions is approximately 47.8% if she gets an attack off. To make things worse, she keeps her Single Target, which means that she only has a chance of debuffing a single enemy at a time. To make things even worse, her True Form costs a rare [[Catfruit|Golden Catfruit Seed]], making her merely a collector's item.

